81640d" :answer-field/key "field-11" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6dd2e619-5980-5c46-8b7c-cffa021d599f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c93810a-cabc-563d-a638-9ea9bc881974" "answer-be6fb967-ed43-5d90-b96a-3dca84b60eb3" "answer-ef44706d-2c2c-519b-99ff-da0d86544b8b" "answer-6dd2e619-5980-5c46-8b7c-cffa021d599f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c93810a-cabc-563d-a638-9ea9bc881974" :answer/id #uuid "1c93810a-cabc-563d-a638-9ea9bc881974" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be6fb967-ed43-5d90-b96a-3dca84b60eb3" :answer/id #uuid "be6fb967-ed43-5d90-b96a-3dca84b60eb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef44706d-2c2c-519b-99ff-da0d86544b8b" :answer/id #uuid "ef44706d-2c2c-519b-99ff-da0d86544b8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dd2e619-5980-5c46-8b7c-cffa021d599f" :answer/id #uuid "6dd2e619-5980-5c46-8b7c-cffa021d599f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d7c03f5-6a85-5299-a2e8-604190d0a4f0" :question/id #uuid "9d7c03f5-6a85-5299-a2e8-604190d0a4f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-363852" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $5$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55735009-9550-5c5e-95a5-2e70fd24c259" "answer-field-b9211c3d-8fb1-578e-8996-7b044b41b0fc" "answer-field-ce1561e8-4f7a-532e-ab2e-ea557ffdb1ee" "answer-field-74fd618f-00ae-5368-bcee-dc76b343da27" "answer-field-7f39c425-4aad-5832-9a14-fa9a082e5395" "answer-field-c389cf18-8eaa-599e-9c81-6eb72de841fe" "answer-field-c5e4f62e-e7e0-53fe-bf6c-de87cefdda4e" "answer-field-d28d15b3-8b99-5ae6-b6f5-1e80a700f429" "answer-field-c7fdd8ac-7488-5735-a1ff-30650f9cc748" "answer-field-32cc1959-e00d-5466-9a3b-6f5b5b9959d8" "answer-field-0e5a83d3-b68d-5e34-9093-7873a4a49b6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55735009-9550-5c5e-95a5-2e70fd24c259" :answer-field/id #uuid "55735009-9550-5c5e-95a5-2e70fd24c259" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-5f746a1d-c18e-537c-bf9b-70a78fed9665" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cba032f6-895a-5d1c-be9d-7c0af4d73d48" "answer-b8c27e7c-81c9-5ce2-82ba-d1fdcedef0d7" "answer-5f746a1d-c18e-537c-bf9b-70a78fed9665" "answer-df08b038-b03c-5634-b70b-39bbe6f3a9f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cba032f6-895a-5d1c-be9d-7c0af4d73d48" :answer/id #uuid "cba032f6-895a-5d1c-be9d-7c0af4d73d48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8c27e7c-81c9-5ce2-82ba-d1fdcedef0d7" :answer/id #uuid "b8c27e7c-81c9-5ce2-82ba-d1fdcedef0d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f746a1d-c18e-537c-bf9b-70a78fed9665" :answer/id #uuid "5f746a1d-c18e-537c-bf9b-70a78fed9665" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df08b038-b03c-5634-b70b-39bbe6f3a9f0" :answer/id #uuid "df08b038-b03c-5634-b70b-39bbe6f3a9f0" :answer/type :